{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-269388",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ 12 & 12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -9 \\\\ 36 & 36\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
